WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:08.900
What’s up, everyone? Jared, again.

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.760
Here at Wisecrack, we adore Assassin’s Creed.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.520
Ubisoft’s hallmark series combines our love for history, science fiction,

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.440
and fast-paced action all into one package.

00:00:18.449 --> 00:00:23.430
Sure, some games have been hilariously glitchy and depressingly underwhelming, but overall,

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:26.359
the series has remained a steadfast favorite of ours.

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:31.020
So when many of you asked for a “Philosophy
of” video, we were really, really tempted.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:33.560
But there’s just one major problem:

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:37.500
while the games include historical titans
like Machiavelli and Benjamin Franklin,

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:40.420
there’s really not much in the way of philosophy, here.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:46.120
Of course, there’s the Assassin’s mantra,
“Nothing is true; everything is permitted."

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:51.460
But no matter how far we dive into the series, this idea is never really developed through the plot.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:56.600
Instead, it’s more or less just lip service that serves as a background for a game about killing popes,

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:59.180
redcoats, pirates, and so on.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:01.610
Yeah, it is insanely fun.

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:05.140
This slightly changes with the arrival of
the Assassin’s Creed movie.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:10.150
Unlike the rest of the series, the vague philosophy of “everything is permitted” has no gameplay

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:11.390
to hide behind.

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:13.660
And when the movie tries to make sense of
this mantra —

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:15.320
"Todo esta permitido."

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:17.380
Well, it doesn’t quite work.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:21.460
So, sit back and let us tell you the story
of how a 1939 Slovenian novel

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.420
became the philosophical backbone for Assassin’s Creed —

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:25.360
sorta.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:28.980
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on Assassin's Creed: What Went Wrong

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:29.980
(Mostly the movie.)

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:31.860
And of course, spoilers ahead!

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.420
“Nothing is true. Everything is permitted.”

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:37.220
If you’ve played any of the games, you’ve heard this phrase,

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:39.940
and it probably got your philosophy sense
tingling.

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:44.951
To explain how this mantra makes little sense
in the games, and no sense in the movie,

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:51.500
we first have to consider where the game lifted it from: Vladimir Bartol’s relatively obscure book,  "Alamut".

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:55.720
As it turns out, "Alamut" is the most translated
Slovenian text in the world,

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:58.200
and honestly, we can’t recommend it enough.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:03.150
It is a gem, full of rich characterization
and deep philosophy.

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:08.350
The novel centers around the eponymous Iranian
castle of Alamut in the eleventh century,

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:11.700
controlled by the Ismaili prophet, Hassan-I Sabbah.

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:17.380
While Ismailism is a real branch of Shia Islam,
and Hassan a real historical figure,

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:19.840
Bartol chose to fictionalize both.

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:24.580
In the book, Hassan’s followers believe he possesses the literal keys to Paradise,

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:29.560
but Hassan himself believes only in rational
thought and empirical truths.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:33.760
In order to control his followers, he deceives them by drugging them with opiates

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:38.800
and delivering them to artfully constructed gardens and harems meant to emulate Heaven.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:42.800
In doing so, Hassan has created a fervent
army of “hashshashin,"

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:45.280
or as we know them today, "assassins."

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:46.900
Hassan’s guiding belief?

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:49.870
“Nothing is true. Everything is permitted."

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:55.340
This mantra is the supposed philosophical underpinning of the entire Assassin’s Creed franchise.

00:02:55.340 --> 00:03:00.960
See, the series explores the age-old conflict between two ideologically opposed, fictionalized groups:

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.950
the Assassins and the Knights Templar.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:09.510
Whereas the Assassins venerate free will,
the Templars are obsessed with enslaving mankind.

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:14.100
To this end, the groups vie for control over
the Apples of Eden, remnants of a lost,

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:18.840
higher civilization that supposedly contain the origins of man’s free will.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:21.100
The Assassins want to safeguard the Apples,

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:25.020
while the Templars want to use them to make mankind obey their will.

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:27.360
We have a similar situation play out in the film,

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:31.820
when convicted murderer Callum Lynch wakes up in the Abstergo Foundation’s laboratory,

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:34.200
a modern day front for the Templars.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:35.960
With the help of a machine called the Animus,

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:37.100
"Prepare the Animus."

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:42.340
Cal must relive the life of his fifteenth century ancestor — an Assassin named Aguilar —

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:43.900
and find the Apple.

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:49.520
For such a rich concept, one might be wondering
why the Assassin’s Creed film is such a train wreck,

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:54.640
or why the original trilogy of Assassin’s Creed games had such an underwhelming ending.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:56.960
While many of you might chalk it all up to
bad writing —

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:59.960
"There's an open menu, but we do recommend the chicken."

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:01.900
— which is definitely part of it,

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:03.740
we think there’s something more going
on.

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:07.440
See, the series never fleshes out the Assassin’s motivations,

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.640
or what really lies behind their conflict with the Templars.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:13.760
These issues are really just symptoms of a larger problem:

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:18.160
the franchise’s confused handling of the philosophy behind "Alamut."

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:23.599
First of all, where "Alamut" sees this mantra
being spouted by a guy trying to enslave people,

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:28.900
Assassin’s Creed decides to assign it to the Assassins, who are allegedly fighting for freedom.

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:34.380
And it's not exactly an uplifting code to live
by, especially for the supposed “good guys."

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:39.140
"Where other men blindly follow the truth, remember..."

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:41.140
"Nothing is true."

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:45.700
"Where other men are limited by their morality or law, remember..."

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:48.540
"Everything is permitted."

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:54.840
If this seems extreme to you, that’s because this, on the surface, seems like an argument for moral nihilism,

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:59.600
in which all moral judgements are false because there’s no underlying truth to them.

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:01.700
"We believe in nothing, Lebowski. Nothing!"

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:03.419
Want to push Grandma down the stairs?

00:05:03.419 --> 00:05:05.900
Sure, go right ahead. Nothing matters!

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:10.720
Although the franchise uses this quote to establish a conflict between free will and slavery,

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:13.600
that’s not what this quote is referring to.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:17.900
If the whole “everything is permitted” thing strikes you as a belief that a villain should hold —

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:19.440
well, there’s good reason for that:

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:25.180
it comes from Hassan-I Sabbah, the Ismaili prophet and chief antagonist of "Alamut."

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:31.640
Hassan is ultimately a tragic figure, a man whose intellect and ambition have robbed him of happiness.

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:36.620
Taking a page of from Descartes, Hassan believes
that emotions, truth, and even God

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:40.840
are all misunderstandings born out of man’s imperfect senses.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:44.659
This crippling realization leaves him feeling
empty and alone.

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:49.720
The only thing that can make him feel alive
again is gambling his own fate and the fate of others,

00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:51.620
which is why he built his army.

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:58.320
To Hassan, nothing is sacred, and therefore,
there is nothing he won’t do in his pursuit of power.

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:01.420
So, what does Hassan do once he discovers that
nothing is true?

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:06.380
He comes to the conclusion that religion isjust the intentional manipulation of the masses

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:07.820
for their own good.

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:12.300
Prophets like Moses, Jesus, and Muhammad recognized
man’s frailty and, out of pity,

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:16.860
crafted concepts such as heaven and paradise to comfort them.

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:21.389
Mankind, according to Hassan, cares nothing
for justice or truth —

00:06:21.389 --> 00:06:24.100
only their own comfort and convenience.

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:30.280
Consequently, he believes that he’s entitled
to use religion to deceive and control his followers,

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:32.720
and in the process, do good by them.

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:39.180
Now, the problem here is that this is the exact credo of the Templars — control the masses for their own good —

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:43.059
even though it's the Assassins that are parroting Hassan’s motto.

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:48.720
These muddled motivations may not matter too much in a game, but it ends up really mattering in a movie,

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:52.980
where the audience needs a clear
division between protagonist and antagonist.

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:56.680
And at the end of the day, why are the Assassins the  good guys?

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:01.660
Aside from the cult-like ritual that opens the film and the constant repetition of their one-line philosophy,

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:05.740
the Assassins Brotherhood
isn’t explored as an organization at all.

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:09.000
After a two hour film, we don’t know anything more about them

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:12.660
than what we learned from the floating text given to us at the movie’s start:

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:16.320
the Templars are bad, and the Assassins are trying to stop them.

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:20.520
This is the only way the movie tries to convince
us that the Assassins are the good guys –

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:24.740
by making sure we know that the Templars are
definitely the villains.

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:27.029
And boy, are the Templars evil.

00:07:27.029 --> 00:07:32.639
Here is a secret, ancient organization disguised
as the multi-billion dollar Abstergo Corporation.

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:35.820
It’s got all the trappings of an elite villainous super group:

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:39.140
a modern, concrete facility complete with white halogen lights,

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:41.640
higher-ups who refer to themselves as elders,

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:45.380
"Our work belongs to the elders. This is their finest hour."

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:47.720
some cookie-cutter lines about controlling the masses,

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:52.080
"The threat remains, while free will exists."

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:53.980
and a leader played by Jeremy Irons.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:58.380
So once again, the Templars are bad, but why
are the Assassins good?

00:07:58.389 --> 00:08:00.694
This is the question we keep coming back to,

00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:05.680
and it goes back to the philosophy informing the conflict between Assassins and Templars.

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:10.600
Because, as the movie reveals, the two are,
ideologically, very similar.

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:14.679
Both sides are just reflections of Hassan-I Sabbah’s ideology.

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:17.280
Although we’re being a little generous to
the Assassins here,

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:22.139
since the movie doesn’t really bother to give them a clear motivation outside the motto they lifted from Hassan.

00:08:22.139 --> 00:08:26.009
In the film, we see the Templars controlling
the masses just like Hassan did.

00:08:26.009 --> 00:08:31.660
They use the Spanish Inquisition as a front to solidify their power and commit some truly heinous crimes:

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:36.240
raiding houses, murdering
innocents, and torching people alive.

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:37.880
Hassan did the same thing,

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:43.660
using the Ismaili doctrine to create a fanatical army that literally tortures its captured enemies.

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:48.480
And while the Templars no longer use religion
to control the masses, they use something else:

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:50.280
a corporation.

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:55.880
"For centuries, we're tried — with religion, with politics, and now consumerism — to eliminate dissent."

00:08:55.880 --> 00:09:00.160
That’s right, Templars control the masses
through consumerism and ease of life.

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:04.300
But even this is another reflection of Hassan’s
dangerous philosophy.

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:08.900
The Supreme Commander of the Ismaili faith
frequently compares his religion to a machine,

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:12.960
one which turns food, shelter, and comfort
into a deadly army.

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:18.120
Having both a protagonist and an antagonist
with the same motivations isn’t just boring,

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:19.720
it’s plain bad.

00:09:19.730 --> 00:09:24.420
Of course, as the movie’s chief source material,
the games are guilty of this as well.

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:28.340
And perhaps, nowhere is this more evident than
in Assassin’s Creed 3.

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:31.700
In the game, Desmond Miles has escaped
control of Abstergo,

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:36.860
but is still using the Animus to access the memories of his 18th century Ancestor, Haytham Kenway.

00:09:36.860 --> 00:09:40.330
For the first couple of hours of the game,
we go through the all the familiar motions:

00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:43.120
climbing on buildings, stabbing people with
our hidden blade,

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:47.160
and chatting with our comrades about how "The Order" is going to make the world a better place.

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:51.500
The twist? Well, this Order is actually the Order of the Templars.

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:53.060
"Wait, what?"

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:55.600
Yep, Haytham is actually a Templar.

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:59.380
But because the Assassin’s ideology, and their motto, is so vague,

00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:03.620
we couldn’t tell the difference between whether we were playing a Templar or an Assassin.

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:07.980
Or, in the first game, Desmond is forced to relive the memories of his 12th century ancestor,

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:09.400
Altaïr Ibn-La’Ahad.

00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:12.580
Altaïr is a high-ranking Assassin who disgraces
himself

00:10:12.580 --> 00:10:16.320
and is forced to redeem himself by assassinating high-ranking Templars.

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:21.160
The twist, here, is that Altaïr’s boss and
the leader of the Assassins, Al Mualim,

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:26.680
has been working with both the Assassins and the Templars to acquire the Apple of Eden for himself.

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:31.540
Like the chapter with Kenway, this shows us how ideologically indistinct the Assassins are.

00:10:31.540 --> 00:10:35.080
Without any real moral or ideological substance behind their motto,

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:37.600
Al Mualim was able to convince the Assassins,

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:42.140
and the player, to kill his enemies by simply labeling them as Templars.

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:47.200
It’s almost as if the Assassins, here, were
like the Ismaili faith being manipulated by Hassan,

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:50.420
except they inexplicably hold the
same view:

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:53.160
“Nothing is true. Everything is permitted."

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:55.600
So here’s the big question, guys and gals.

00:10:55.610 --> 00:10:59.740
We have a franchise whose central conflict
is kind of a mess.

00:10:59.740 --> 00:11:04.050
So, why do we love scaling some of the tallest
buildings across Europe and the Middle East,

00:11:04.050 --> 00:11:08.850
while we nearly pulled our hair out watching
Fassbender deliver another uninspired line?

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:13.180
Well, the best we can come up with is the difference
in agency between films and video games.

00:11:13.180 --> 00:11:18.880
In films, we’re not active participants, so it’s mainly the narrative that keeps us interested.

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:23.600
Sure, choreography, lighting, set design,
and a whole host of other factors keep us tuned in,

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:26.920
but ultimately, it’s the story that we watch for.

00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:30.780
As such, films mostly live and die by the
quality of their narratives.

00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:34.860
Whether it’s The Shawshank Redemption or The Bee Movie, a film primarily succeeds

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:40.080
by having a dynamic protagonist whose journey has a beginning, middle, and end.

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:43.100
Video games, however, are different because
we play them.

00:11:43.100 --> 00:11:48.000
They don’t need a story to be engaging,
as long the gameplay mechanics are enjoyable.

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:51.660
Wonder how a game like Flappy Bird can become a worldwide sensation overnight,

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:54.340
generating $50,000 a day in revenue?

00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:56.700
Simple, stupidly addictive gameplay.

00:11:56.700 --> 00:11:59.780
Which means we’re naturally more forgiving
when a video game slips up.

00:11:59.780 --> 00:12:01.960
Don’t want to watch the plot unfold?

00:12:01.960 --> 00:12:05.880
Push A, skip the cutscene, and go back
to slashing throats.

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:10.100
This is why we think the Assassin’s Creed
franchise stands up so well as a game,

00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:13.160
when it falls right on its face as a film.

00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:18.440
In the movie, we couldn’t care less about
Cal or Aguilar scaling buildings or assassinating priests,

00:12:18.440 --> 00:12:23.080
precisely because the film doesn’t
bother to create an engaging narrative to hook us.

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:27.540
Would I rather watch two emotionally dead-eyed characters hop between buildings

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:31.300
like a Chinese acrobat, or would I rather control a character, and do it myself?

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:33.840
I think the choice here is obvious.

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:36.740
So, while the games will forever remain a favorite of ours,

00:12:36.740 --> 00:12:40.340
we think we’ll pass if they ever make another Assassin’s Creed movie.

00:12:40.340 --> 00:12:42.400
Until next time, stay good, guys.

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:43.240
Peace.

